  "promptExtra": "FINISH-RUN CONTEXT (2026-08-15): stages KS0, KS1, KS2 and KS5 - and KS3.1-KS3.3 - are DELIVERED AND MERGED on feat/karvansara; the run confirms them with phase gates and spends sessions only on what remains: KS3.4 round 8, KS3.5, KS9, KS10.1 and KS10.2. Per-checkpoint contracts live in plans/karvansara/contracts/. KS10.3 stays owner-only. The engine driving you is a mid-era snapshot, 0.4.1-alpha.0.49+9bf2742 - KS0, KS1 and KS2 are IN it, KS5's approve semantics and KS3's preflight are NOT - so the traps below marked -until KS0.x lands- are fixed in the driver, but keep the habits anyway; never reinstall it (the owner does that at KS10.3) and exercise your changes only through your fresh build.\n\nTRAPS THIS REPO HAS ALREADY PAID FOR, in order of cost:\n0. THIS REPO'S .conductor/ IS THE LIVE STATE OF THE RUN DRIVING YOU. The claim and note verbs (conductor task, note, bug, bg) and the read-only verbs (status, task --list) are yours; NEVER aim a run-control verb (run, pause, resume, abort, approve, plan set, plan reload, goto, skip, retry-stage, rollback, kill) at this repo. Any live-run proof spawns YOUR build against a scratch repo with its own tiny plan and its own state dir - for example under the temp directory - never against C:/code/conductor.\n1. NEVER run tools/install.ps1 or overwrite the published engine. The conductor driving you runs from that published copy. The owner reinstalls at KS10.3, not before.\n2. The conductor on PATH is the published engine driving you - NOT your working tree. Exercise YOUR changes through the fresh build: dotnet run --project src/Conductor -- VERB (or go run ./cmd/... in face-go). Testing a new verb through the PATH shim only proves the published engine lacks it. Exception by design: task claims go through the PATH copy, because they target the driving run.\n3. A SECOND CONDUCTOR RUN MAY SHARE THIS MACHINE, in another repo, and you can break it without touching it. Never kill a conductor.exe, conductor-face.exe or stray dotnet process by pid: check its command line with Get-CimInstance Win32_Process and leave anything whose path is not this repo alone, no matter how stale it looks. Give every scratch rig its own port and its own state dir, and read the port back from the rig's own discovery file instead of assuming a default.\n4. THE CONDUCTOR_PLAN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE BEATS THE CWD (bug 20, fixed in KS0.3 but live until that lands). Every conductor verb you run in a scratch rig must pass -p explicitly, and clear CONDUCTOR_PLAN in any shell you spawn. The F0 to R0 phantom stages sitting in plans/karvan/CORE-TRACKER.md today are the scar this trap leaves: a scratch rig quietly edited the DRIVING run's plan.\n5. THE CATALOGUE MINTS DUPLICATES UNDER NEW PLAN SLUGS (until KS0.1 lands): legacy-db import keys on plan slug, so read-only verbs in this old repo can grow the catalogue. Before diagnosing any duplicate run row, read imported.json in the state home. Never repair duplicates by hand-SQL - KS0.1 ships the repair pass, with a backup, and the payesh harvest must stay green on the deduped view.\n6. FOUR PHANTOM RUNNING ROWS exist in the catalogue until KS0.2 closes them via the new verb. They are evidence, not garbage: KS0.2's acceptance is closing them through the CLI. Do not delete them, do not hand-edit SQL - the WATCH-HANDOFF.md hand-SQL procedure is the thing KS0.2 exists to retire.\n7. NO GATE MAY REBUILD THE RUNNING ENGINE (bug 16, fixed in KS0.3 but live until that lands). This plan's gates build the solution, which is safe; never add a gate or a proof step that runs tools/install.ps1, publishes to the engine's install path, or invokes the tools/gates battery scripts that rebuild conductor.exe in place.\n8. A literal brace token in a file under this plan's templates directory KILLS THE ENGINE - the template text is validated for unresolved placeholders, the refusal goes to stderr only, and it fires when a stage first renders that template. After any template edit, sweep it: only real placeholder names may appear, and a doubled brace emits a literal one.\n9. Keep the escalation token out of the tracker's handoff block unless you are escalating right now. The match is a plain substring, so prose describing the convention parks the run as hard as raising one, and the park then hot-loops and floods Telegram (KS2.6 fixes the flood; the substring match stays). Write escalation in prose; the literal form is the word HUMAN followed by a colon.\n10. face-go: pad plain text then style, never width-format an ANSI string; clamp with MaxWidth and MaxHeight; goldens pin UTC and live in face-go/internal/tui/testdata/golden - regenerate them only in a separate rebaseline commit; read face-go/STYLE.md and adr 0006 before any face change. KS2.7 and KS2.8 are face-heavy: the pane viewport (panescroll.go) is the ONLY scroll idiom - never add a bespoke scroll integer.\n11. KS9 GITHUB WORK TARGETS A SCRATCH REPO ONLY. Never create, edit or close issues on shaahink/conductor or any real repository during proofs; the owner does the first real backfill at KS10.3. The token comes from SecretsStore or CONDUCTOR_GITHUB_TOKEN; nothing inbound, ever - no webhook, no poll of issue edits, no state read back from GitHub into the run.\n12. Orphaned dotnet test hosts flake the full suite. Stopping one is sanctioned ONLY after trap 3's command-line check proves it belongs to this repo; never kill a conductor process, yours or anyone's.\n13. PowerShell tooling targets Windows PowerShell 5.1 and must stay ASCII-only - em-dashes have torn string literals here before. Never call gh --jq from PowerShell when the filter contains double quotes; fetch raw JSON and ConvertFrom-Json.\n14. Commit per checkpoint with the repo's trailer convention and push feat/karvansara. Go sources are checked out LF; keep them that way. If you move a file this run depends on - the plan's readOrder target, the tracker, a template - update the plan and reload it in the same checkpoint, or the next session reads nothing.\n15. This era's token ceiling was measured, not guessed: 32M with the nudge at 22.4M, fitted to claude-opus-5 on karvan-core's own run.db - 16.8M tokens and 13.24 dollars per checkpoint, zero rollovers in 30 costed sessions. If you believe the ceiling is wrong, say so with a measurement in the handoff - do not work around it by splitting a checkpoint you were not asked to split. A model change invalidates the pair; that re-derivation belongs to the owner, not to a session.\n16. THE FIELD GUIDE IS A LIVE PUBLIC SITE. C:/code/conductor-site is shaahink/payesh, and a push to its main auto-deploys payesh.vercel.app to the world. It is declared as a satellite for ONE reason: KS10.2 re-runs its harvest against the deduped catalogue to prove KS0.1 did not break it. Work there on a branch, open a PR, and stop; the owner merges at KS10.3. No stage before KS10 touches that repo at all. Its figures are recomputed from the run store, never typed in, and anonymisation fails closed - if a harvest wants to emit a literal where an evidence key belongs, that is a bug to report, not a value to paste.",
